  "note": {
    "title": "RL paper club: random rewards and pass at k",
    "content": "",
    "note_type": "meeting",
    "transcript": [
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-1",
        "text": "Okay, paper club. Random rewards, the spurious rewards paper.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061203423,
        "startMs": 3423,
        "endMs": 7935,
        "speaker": "speaker_1",
        "speakerName": "Nadia",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-2",
        "text": "Right. Spurious rewards: RLVR with random rewards on the Qwen math checkpoints.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061209329,
        "startMs": 9329,
        "endMs": 15673,
        "speaker": "speaker_2",
        "speakerName": "Felix",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-3",
        "text": "Random rewards, wrong-answer rewards, format rewards. On Qwen, all of these spurious rewards raise the math scores.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061216409,
        "startMs": 16409,
        "endMs": 25115,
        "speaker": "speaker_2",
        "speakerName": "Felix",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-4",
        "text": "And on Llama and OLMo, the spurious rewards mostly do nothing for the math.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061225763,
        "startMs": 25763,
        "endMs": 32824,
        "speaker": "speaker_3",
        "speakerName": "Wen",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-5",
        "text": "So random rewards only help a base checkpoint that already has the habit.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061234250,
        "startMs": 34250,
        "endMs": 40598,
        "speaker": "speaker_1",
        "speakerName": "Nadia",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-6",
        "text": "The Qwen habit is code reasoning. Qwen thinks in Python, and the random rewards make it think in Python more often.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061241384,
        "startMs": 41384,
        "endMs": 53228,
        "speaker": "speaker_2",
        "speakerName": "Felix",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-7",
        "text": "How does a random reward push anything at all?",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061253901,
        "startMs": 53901,
        "endMs": 58151,
        "speaker": "speaker_3",
        "speakerName": "Wen",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-8",
        "text": "The clipping in GRPO biases the update.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061259021,
        "startMs": 59021,
        "endMs": 62976,
        "speaker": "speaker_2",
        "speakerName": "Felix",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-9",
        "text": "Clipping favours the habits the checkpoint already likes, so even random rewards sharpen them.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061263947,
        "startMs": 63947,
        "endMs": 71425,
        "speaker": "speaker_2",
        "speakerName": "Felix",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-10",
        "text": "Then every RLVR paper on Qwen needs a random rewards baseline.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061272463,
        "startMs": 72463,
        "endMs": 77997,
        "speaker": "speaker_1",
        "speakerName": "Nadia",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-11",
        "text": "Next, pass at k, from Yue and colleagues.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061278684,
        "startMs": 78684,
        "endMs": 82862,
        "speaker": "speaker_1",
        "speakerName": "Nadia",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-12",
        "text": "Right. Yue and colleagues sample the base model hundreds of times, against the trained model.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061284489,
        "startMs": 84489,
        "endMs": 91659,
        "speaker": "speaker_3",
        "speakerName": "Wen",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-13",
        "text": "At one sample, the trained model wins. At hundreds of samples, the base model solves more problems.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061292414,
        "startMs": 92414,
        "endMs": 100391,
        "speaker": "speaker_3",
        "speakerName": "Wen",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-14",
        "text": "So training narrows the sampling. The reasoning paths were already in the base model.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061301063,
        "startMs": 101063,
        "endMs": 108687,
        "speaker": "speaker_2",
        "speakerName": "Felix",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-15",
        "text": "Did they test distillation too?",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061309406,
        "startMs": 109406,
        "endMs": 112006,
        "speaker": "speaker_1",
        "speakerName": "Nadia",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-16",
        "text": "Yes, and distillation did widen it.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061312953,
        "startMs": 112953,
        "endMs": 116029,
        "speaker": "speaker_3",
        "speakerName": "Wen",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-17",
        "text": "Distillation from a stronger teacher model raised pass at k. Sampling more from the base model never did that.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061316824,
        "startMs": 116824,
        "endMs": 126454,
        "speaker": "speaker_3",
        "speakerName": "Wen",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-18",
        "text": "That fits. Training polishes, a teacher model adds.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061327513,
        "startMs": 127513,
        "endMs": 131723,
        "speaker": "speaker_2",
        "speakerName": "Felix",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-19",
        "text": "Does the code reasoning habit show up in the bigger Qwen checkpoints?",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061332697,
        "startMs": 132697,
        "endMs": 138907,
        "speaker": "speaker_1",
        "speakerName": "Nadia",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-20",
        "text": "No idea.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061340002,
        "startMs": 140002,
        "endMs": 141252,
        "speaker": "speaker_2",
        "speakerName": "Felix",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      },
      {
        "id": "seg-musvjbtk-21",
        "text": "Okay, back to spurious rewards. The spurious rewards paper goes on the reading group list, with a random rewards control of our own.",
        "source": "mic",
        "timestamp": 1791061342385,
        "startMs": 142385,
        "endMs": 153990,
        "speaker": "speaker_1",
        "speakerName": "Nadia",
        "speakerIsPlaceholder": false,
        "speakerStatus": "locked",
        "speakerLocked": false
      }
    ],
    "enhanced_content": "Nadia, Felix and Wen read two papers on reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR): the spurious rewards paper and Yue et al. on pass@k.\n\n## Threads\n- **Spurious rewards.** On Qwen math checkpoints, random, wrong-answer and format rewards all raise math scores; on Llama and OLMo they mostly do not. The paper's explanation is code reasoning that Qwen already has, amplified by the clipping in GRPO.\n- **Pass@k.** RLVR checkpoints win at pass@1, but at large k the base checkpoint solves more problems: RLVR narrows the search rather than adding reasoning paths. Distillation from a stronger teacher did widen it.\n\n## Decisions Made\n- Every RLVR result on Qwen needs a random-reward baseline.\n- The spurious rewards paper goes on the reading group list.\n\n## Follow-ups\n- Whether the code reasoning habit shows up in the larger Qwen checkpoints.",
